raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Did the Democratic campaign ad labeling Trump a convicted criminal worsen Democrats’ views of people with criminal records more generally?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Thus, this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of\npeople with criminal records",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Did the ad change how well Democrats felt represented by their elected officials or how strongly they believed those officials were motivated by public service rather than self-interest?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well\nDemocrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The first of these was to test the impact of exposure to the Trump advertisement\non Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records, as well as their perceptions of elected officials’ motivations\n(i.e., the extent to which they are motivated by serving the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.)",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Could hearing first from a formerly incarcerated elected official change Democrats’ responses to the ad and offset its possible harms?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, this study seeks to understand whether\nproviding a counterexample to Trump – highlighting the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the\npropagation of this label by the Democratic party – may counter-act potential negative impacts of the Party’s Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A secondary question was whether the effects differed for respondents who had been involved in the justice system themselves or affected through a loved one.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "As specified in the pre-registered, pre-analysis plan, supplemental analyses will also explore potential heterogeneity by\nsystem-impact status (i.e., whether someone identifies as having previously been involved in the system or impacted by\nthe system through their loved one’s involvement.)",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The experiment points to a trade-off: the attack ad made Democrats feel better represented, but its negative spillover onto people with criminal records was substantially larger than its apparent representational benefit.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results suggest that exposure\nto the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved\nperceived feelings of representation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "While the potential “positive” effects of exposure to the Trump advertisement signal that this may benefit the Democratic\nparty, the magnitude of these effects (approximately 0.30 to 0.34 in absolute value SD units) are considerably smaller than\nthe advertisement’s effects on perceptions of individuals with criminal records (approximately 0.83)",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The near-offset of the harm suggests an alternative worth testing: acknowledge Trump’s convictions while giving prominence to the perspectives of public servants who have been through the justice system.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly\nameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of\nindividuals with criminal records.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Instead, a viable, alternate route for political messaging\nduring the 2024 presidential election could have been to acknowledge, but juxtapose, the nature of Trump’s convictions and his\nbroadcasted attitude about his convictions with effective counter-narratives.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study recruited 2,500 participants through Prolific on July 12–13, 2025, targeting English-speaking Democrats living in the United States.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform that is commonly utilized for conducting survey-based\nresearch, which has been demonstrated as a reliable platform for obtaining high-quality data.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "2,500 participants were\nrecruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025 [60]. This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within\nthe United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The final sample contained 2,439 respondents after exclusions for failed attention checks, duplicate IDs, invalid or missing completion codes, and suspected bot responses.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Of the 2,729 total attempts, 2,481 were retained after dropping those who failed the attention check,\nwere duplicate observations based on stored Prolific identifier embedded data, or provided the wrong completion code",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Approximately 1.69% of responses post-removal of duplicates, incorrect/no codes, and failed attention checks were\nflagged as not being a likely human response, and were also dropped.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "leaving the remaining, final analytical sample size of 2,439 participants.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analytic sample was 59.4% women, 70.6% White, 60.8% college graduates or above, and 21.4% self-identified as affected by the justice system. The average age was 43.4 years.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "a majority of which (59.4%) identified as women, with\n36.8% identifying as men, and 3.8% identifying as non-binary and/or another gender identity. Most participants were White\n(70.6%)",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Most participants had a four-year degree or higher (60.8%) and 21.4% of the sample identified as being system-impacted.\nThe average age of participants was 43.4 years old.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random assignment determined what respondents saw before the main outcomes: neither message in the control arm (813), the ad alone (814), or the counter-narrative followed by the ad (812). The control arm saw the ad and narrative after the main outcomes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the\nControl group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "the Control group, which presented all outcome questions first, followed by the Trump advertisement stimulus (and associated questions related to the advertisement), followed by the counter-narrative.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The Trump Ad Alone group\npresented participants with the advertisement (and its associated questions) first, followed by the remaining outcome\nquestions, and subsequently the counter-narrative.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Finally, the Counter-Narrative First group presented the Trump advertisement (and its associated questions) only after being shown the counter-narrative. Then the outcomes were presented.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad stimulus was the same across arms: a description of the 2024 Democratic campaign’s use of the ad and an image of Trump’s mugshot labeled CONVICTED CRIMINAL.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Regardless of when participants were presented with the Trump advertisement, the content was (importantly) constant\nacross treatment arms.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Below this descriptive text, participants were presented with one of the images\nutilized in advertisements within the 2024 presidential election, specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which\nwas labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The text-only counter-narrative came from a lightly edited Michael Lyle article in the Nevada Current. It presented Jovan Jackson’s experience as a formerly incarcerated advocate and lawmaker, including his objection to the campaign’s framing and his emphasis on rehabilitation and accountability.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The counter-narrative, on the other hand, provided no visual stimulus, but rather an excerpt for participants to read.\nThis excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV\nLegislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024 [55].",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The text was lightly edited for\nbrevity, to remove mention of the state in which this individual is an elected official, and their first name, to more narrowly\ndefine the counter-narrative treatment.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Jackson found the contrast hurtful and undercut the work he had done since being incarcerated. He said there is a clear difference between a formerly incarcerated person who sought rehabilitation, served\ntheir time and was released, and Trump.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "As the state considers what justice reforms to pursue and how those laws are written, Jackson said he intends to provide representation for those impacted by the justice system",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Two single-item, seven-point agreement measures captured different views of representation: whether diverse life experiences strengthen democracy and whether their value depends on effective advocacy for constituents.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Both questions asked participants to state the extent to which they agreed with each statement presented on a seven-point Likert scale, ranging from\n“Strongly disagree” to “Strongly agree.”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives with a wide array of life experiences is crucial to strengthening our democracy,”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives\nwith a wide array of life experiences is really only beneficial when our representatives effectively advocate for the needs of\ntheir constituents.”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The other four outcomes were averaged and standardized indices: five items on people with records, three on federal Democratic representation, four on officials’ motives, and two on ad support and perceived effectiveness.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The remaining four outcomes are measured as indices, averaged over each question included in the index, and subsequently standardized for ease of interpretation.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The perceptions of individuals with criminal records outcome included\nan index, where participants were presented with five statements",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The perceived quality of Democratic representation in government index included three items",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The motives index included four questions, asking participants the extent to which they felt that\nelected officials were motivated to serve the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The two items were again measured the same seven-point\nLikert agreement scale and included: a) “This is an effective advertisement;” and b) “I support use of this advertisement.”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey checked whether respondents reported an emotional reaction to the narrative. Those who saw it first reported a stronger reaction than respondents in either of the other arms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Lastly, a manipulation check was included after exposure to the\nnarrative, specifically asking participants to state the extent to which they agreed with the statement, “I had an emotional\nreaction to this narrative.”",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "the\nCounter-Narrative First group exhibited significantly greater emotional reaction to the narrative than either of the other\ntwo treatment arms",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The two preregistered contrasts were narrative-before-ad versus ad alone and ad alone versus no ad before the outcomes. The author estimated mean differences with least-squares regressions and HC-2 standard errors, reporting models both with and without covariates. Balance tests did not detect significant differences for the arm pairs being compared.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the first (Hypothesis Set 1) corresponds with testing the impact of exposure to\nperspective-getting counter-narrative immediately before exposure to the Trump ad, relative to only being presented\nwith the Trump ad. The second set (Hypothesis Set 2) includes tests of the advertisement’s impact, relative to no\nexposure to the advertisement prior to answering outcome questions.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error\nestimators, HC-2, were utilized. Both the treatment indicator only and the primary models (which include covariates, as\npre-specified), are reported for transparency.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "There were no statistically significant differences\nbetween either the Control and Trump Ad Alone groups, nor the Trump Ad Alone and Counter-Narrative First group.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Seeing the ad alone lowered Democrats’ ratings of people with criminal records by about 0.84 standard deviations relative to answering before seeing the ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive\nperceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Seeing Jackson’s counter-narrative first improved attitudes toward people with records by about 0.83 standard deviations relative to seeing the ad alone, nearly offsetting the ad’s negative effect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated,\nsystem-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated ( β̂2 = 0.833, whereas β̂0 = −0.835).",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad alone improved perceived representation but reduced the value respondents placed on diversity of life experience among elected officials.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Significant differences were also found – counter to hypotheses – for the effect of exposure to the Trump advertisement on valuing diversity in life experiences amongst elected\nofficials. It is possible that exposure to the Trump advertisement encouraged individuals to agree less in the value\nof diversity of life experiences",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Compared with the ad alone, the counter-narrative increased the value respondents placed on diverse life experiences in government, but lowered their ratings of federal Democratic representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the initial exposure to the counter-narrative demonstrating a 0.79 SD unit increase in the perceived value of diversity\nof life experience as an important component of strengthening democracy, relative to those only presented with the Trump\nadvertisement before answering outcome questions ( β̂2 = 0.790, SE = 0.047).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased quality of Democratic representation in federal government, relative to just Trump advertisement exposure ( β̂2 = −0.332, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The counter-narrative reduced overall support for the ad. When the two index items were examined separately, explicit support fell significantly, but perceived effectiveness did not.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = −0.172 SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Both coefficients are negative,\nincluding that of the item “This is an effective advertisement” and “I support the use of this advertisement”, though only the\nexplicit support for the advertisement item was statistically significant.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The findings are preliminary evidence about this Democratic messaging strategy, not evidence that the same effects hold in other populations or settings.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this work should be considered as preliminary evidence of potential effects of the Democratic Party’s messaging strategy, and a line of work that warrants far more empirical attention than it has received.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Relatedly, the extent to which these results would replicate in other contexts and in less-transparent designs is unclear,\nas is the durability of effects.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Without a narrative-only arm, the experiment cannot isolate the effect of the narrative by itself or establish whether the ad altered that standalone effect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the\nimpact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "utilizing a negative campaign advertisement could moderate the effect of the counter-narrative perspective intervention,\ndepending upon one’s personality traits, amongst other potential factors [76].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Because the same labeled mugshot was always used, the study cannot separate the effect of the image from that of the stigmatizing label.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "as the advertisement (i.e., Trump’s mugshot with the words ‘convicted criminal’ imposed on the\nimage) was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from\nthe stigmatizing terminology imposed",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The online survey cannot rule out AI-assisted responses; how often typical panel respondents use AI agents for closed-ended surveys is unclear.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it is also incredibly important to acknowledge",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "Still, the propensity for the standard online survey taker to utilize AI agents to\ncomplete close-ended surveys is not well understood.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants may have answered in socially desirable ways because of the survey design. The emotional-reaction check reduces but does not eliminate that concern.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in\noutcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "The manipulation check, namely the fact that the\nCounter-Narrative First group exhibited significantly greater emotional reaction to the narrative than either of the other\ntwo treatment arms, which were primed with the Trump advertisement before the counter-narrative, helps alleviate some\nconcerns but does not rule this out.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The single survey session does not show how long either effect lasts or whether repeated exposure would change it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative\nprime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist; b) how may effects vary by single versus repeated\nexposure to the advertisement and/or the counter-narrative prime?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study measured reported attitudes and perceptions, not whether the messages changed actual decisions or behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey was run after the election outcome was known, so it does not establish that voters would have responded the same way during the campaign.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively\nduring the campaign. Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question, perhaps especially so\nfor the advertisement’s impacts in particular.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The measures did not capture all dimensions of representation, and the index measuring attitudes toward people with criminal records had only adequate reliability.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, there are myriad aspects feeling represented (e.g.,",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "in terms of policy position, symbolic and active representation on myriad dimensions of identity, etc.), that may be additionally important to explore, and given the reliability statistics for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index\nwas satisfactory but not necessarily strong, utilizing a more robust set of measures is an important next step.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analysis does not test whether the messages changed racial attitudes, which were measured before exposure and used as covariates.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "racial attitudes were measured as covariates and presented before stimuli; however, understanding the potential for activation of negative stereotypes related to those with criminal records could very likely impact Democrats’ racial attitudes,\ngiven the racist stereotypes that are propogated in relation to criminal justice system impact.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The experiment does not settle whether the narrative reduced stigma broadly or instead led respondents to view some people with criminal records as more deserving than others.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While it is possible that the intervention constructs a new category of deservingness, rather than reducing stigma, the\nempirical relationship between attitudes and personal connection suggests that reduction of stigma is more likely [55–57].\nStill, future research should clarify this relationship more deeply.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The effects on beliefs about officials’ public-serving motives did not survive Bonferroni correction in either contrast.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the\npublic-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the public-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The narrative-first comparison did not establish a change in whether respondents saw diversity of experience as beneficial only when paired with effective advocacy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "H1E was unsupported, namely there were\nno significant differences between the Counter-Narrative First and Trump Ad Alone groups on the outcome related to the\ncomplexity of representation ( β̂2 = −0.062, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The author treats the item-level, demographic, and subgroup analyses as secondary and cautions against reading them as firmly as the primary tests.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Supplemental analyses are provided to help clarify findings and explore results that were identified as secondary analyses. Thus, while this means findings in this section should be interpreted with caution, these results are presented to\nprovide transparency and aid interpretation of this study’s findings.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper extends social categorization theory to campaign messaging: assigning a salient figure such as Trump to a group can make him an exemplar whose perceived traits spill over onto other group members.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such\nlabels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions\nfrom Trump, such that perceptions of individuals with records would be worsened\namongst Democrats after exposure to the advertisement, including its affixed, “convicted criminal” label.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study combines social categorization’s focus on exemplars with perspective-getting research: it uses a dedicated, formerly incarcerated official’s own account as a counterexample to the ad’s framing.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, this counter-narrative\nutilized perspective-getting, sharing the experience of a state Assemblymember who was previously incarcerated (i.e., has\na criminal record,) and is dedicated to using their platform to serve the public and advocate for system-impacted populations being represented in government.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "including members of an outgroup, which informed the inclusion of the counter-narrative, given prior literature’s notes\nof its propensity to significantly shift perceptions [67–70].",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper tentatively applies prospect theory’s idea of a reference point to perceived representation: seeing Trump in the ad may make other elected officials look better by comparison.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Collectively, these results may\nsimply be Prospect Theory in action: Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better [73].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Grüning and Schubert found that anger-inducing ads increased support and encouraged action among members of the party running them. Harney builds on that claim by asking whether a Democratic attack ad can improve Democrats’ perceptions of their own party’s representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For example, one study found that anger-inducing political advertisements bolstered\nsupport and encouraged action specifically amongst members of the party producing the advertisement [33].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "These results also lend weight to the potential mechanism through which the Democratic Party could have sought\nto bolster their support, as providing the counter-narrative also worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic\nrepresentation, relative to the Trump advertisement alone.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
